description:
## Finding

`pkix-lint/src/lib.rs:634`:

```rust
debug_assert!({
    let mut ids: Vec<_> = ...;
    ids.sort_unstable();   // <-- mutates inside debug_assert!
    ...
});
```

Clippy: `clippy::debug_assert_with_mut_call`.

## Why it matters

`debug_assert!` is **compiled out** in release builds. Any side-effect inside it (including a `Vec::sort_unstable` call) disappears in release. While the surrounding expression is just for the assertion's truth value, the pattern is fragile — a future edit could hoist the `let mut ids` outside and create a debug/release behavior split that's hard to spot.

## Context

Need to read the actual block to know whether the mutation is inside the assertion's value-producing expression or as a setup statement. Either way, splitting setup from assertion is the safer pattern.

## Fix

Hoist the setup outside:

```rust
let mut ids: Vec<_> = ...;
ids.sort_unstable();
debug_assert!(ids.windows(2).all(|w| w[0] != w[1])); // or whatever the actual check is
```

This way the sort runs in both debug and release.

If the sort is *only* needed for the assertion (i.e., the post-sort check), use `cfg(debug_assertions)`:

```rust
#[cfg(debug_assertions)]
{
    let mut ids: Vec<_> = ...;
    ids.sort_unstable();
    assert!(...);
}
```

## Priority

P4 — defensive coding nit. Look at the actual block; if the mutation is purely for the assertion, the current code is benign but lint-flagged.
